<urn:kg:ofev:chunk:7dbf2957-c600-41fa-b0d3-1718a868a054> a kg:Chunk ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_Brand-Book_Nov-2015.pdf" ;
    kg:chunk_id "7dbf2957-c600-41fa-b0d3-1718a868a054" ;
    kg:chunk_index 67 ;
    kg:extraction_method "docling" ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 12 ;
    kg:section_id "38b8e81a-af41-4c3a-9e9d-3ca01edcf351" ;
    kg:source_document_id "7c99d579-1450-4667-9bc5-7d13421ddab2" ;
    kg:source_document_type "brand_book" ;
    kg:source_file_name "OFEV_Brand-Book_Nov-2015.pdf" ;
    kg:source_hash "60c72e8a9043dc5d2f8a2c353cb9fca286b1cf740583be3592244b23309d1338" ;
    kg:text """The VARGATEF ®  logo is provided for countries that will launch nintedanib under the brand name VARGATEF ®  for both the IPF and lung cancer indications. These countries are required to use the same brand name for both indications; therefore, use of the brand name OFEV ® is not permitted.

GLoBAL LoGo

<!-- image -->""" ;
    kg:token_count 80 .

<urn:kg:ofev:chunk:7e9163a3-1bf1-411f-af8f-c549695f92dd> a kg:Chunk ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_Brand-Book_Nov-2015.pdf" ;
    kg:chunk_id "7e9163a3-1bf1-411f-af8f-c549695f92dd" ;
    kg:chunk_index 65 ;
    kg:extraction_method "docling" ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 10 ;
    kg:section_id "3dbd4263-7db7-437f-a6c4-412c6c8916d0" ;
    kg:source_document_id "7c99d579-1450-4667-9bc5-7d13421ddab2" ;
    kg:source_document_type "brand_book" ;
    kg:source_file_name "OFEV_Brand-Book_Nov-2015.pdf" ;
    kg:source_hash "3b522c1d51b9488d641abc8ac89ae9566f89dc76043ee2aead6c70d7e4c3d8c7" ;
    kg:text "Brand identity assets are the tools that we use to define and communicate a brand's unique characteristics. They include visual elements such as a logo, color, and type, as well as verbal and behavioral elements such as tone of voice, style, and manner. The OFEV ® brand identity assets have been carefully developed to work together to create a bold, charismatic, and memorable visual impression." ;
    kg:token_count 99 .

<urn:kg:ofev:chunk:7f4c28d0-af28-4418-bd23-b5c318f06506> a kg:Chunk ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:chunk_id "7f4c28d0-af28-4418-bd23-b5c318f06506" ;
    kg:chunk_index 73 ;
    kg:extraction_method "docling" ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 11 ;
    kg:section_id "5476add7-f4f8-4a10-b4b4-82947a98ff3d" ;
    kg:source_document_id "c0db5dd8-583e-47bb-9b75-58402a32791c" ;
    kg:source_document_type "prescribing_information" ;
    kg:source_file_name "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:source_hash "f87b27e701ef58a0f8d8a27681df71e4db2dcf8337f76dc9fef2a5f880aa84fb" ;
    kg:text """Based on findings from animal studies and its mechanism of action [see Clinical Pharmacology (12.1)] , OFEV can cause fetal harm when administered to a pregnant woman. There are no data on the use of OFEV during pregnancy. In animal studies of pregnant rats and rabbits treated during organogenesis, nintedanib caused embryo-fetal deaths and structural abnormalities at less than (rats) and approximately 5 times (rabbits) the maximum recommended human dose [see Data] . Advise pregnant women of the potential risk to a fetus.

The estimated background risk of major birth defects and miscarriage for the indicated population is unknown. In the U.S. general population, the estimated background risk of major birth defects is 2% to 4% and miscarriage in clinically recognized pregnancies is 15% to 20%.""" ;
    kg:token_count 200 .

<urn:kg:ofev:chunk:7f6325ff-5fe3-4af6-94fc-0b44bb542e0a> a kg:Chunk ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:chunk_id "7f6325ff-5fe3-4af6-94fc-0b44bb542e0a" ;
    kg:chunk_index 106 ;
    kg:extraction_method "docling" ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 17 ;
    kg:section_id "a058e191-7bb7-4ed5-a7ba-f3ac3527d332" ;
    kg:source_document_id "c0db5dd8-583e-47bb-9b75-58402a32791c" ;
    kg:source_document_type "prescribing_information" ;
    kg:source_file_name "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:source_hash "da3db5f6990121bb3080882c9eebd24dc2a14131ee2674331fab2284fc73f3eb" ;
    kg:text """Healthy volunteers received a single dose of 150 mg nintedanib before and after multiple dosing of 125 mg bosentan twice daily at steady state. Coadministration of nintedanib with bosentan did not alter the pharmacokinetics of nintedanib.

Nintedanib displays a pH-dependent solubility profile with increased solubility at acidic pH less than 3. However, in the clinical trials, coadministration with proton pump inhibitors or histamine H2 antagonists did not influence the exposure (trough concentrations) of nintedanib.

In in vitro studies, nintedanib was shown not to be a substrate of OATP-1B1, OATP-1B3, OATP-2B1, OCT-2, MRP-2, or BCRP. In vitro studies also showed that nintedanib was a substrate of OCT-1; these findings are considered to be of low clinical relevance.""" ;
    kg:token_count 194 .

<urn:kg:ofev:chunk:7f9fcc2a-56f4-4b36-a7c6-f0b9dbb4f7f6> a kg:Chunk ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_Brand-Book_Nov-2015.pdf" ;
