<urn:kg:ofev:chunk:c8633e11-c806-45b0-9dbe-52af82db8b21> a kg:Chunk ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:chunk_id "c8633e11-c806-45b0-9dbe-52af82db8b21" ;
    kg:chunk_index 85 ;
    kg:extraction_method "docling" ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 13 ;
    kg:section_id "d02488c4-6e6b-4e92-8028-b5a0d3304adb" ;
    kg:source_document_id "c0db5dd8-583e-47bb-9b75-58402a32791c" ;
    kg:source_document_type "prescribing_information" ;
    kg:source_file_name "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:source_hash "e88b1fbf832d36b7289a98eaca469ecce7b18464c536bff31f565fa9f1ba0f85" ;
    kg:text "Nintedanib is predominantly eliminated via biliary/fecal excretion (greater than 90%). In a PK study performed in patients with hepatic impairment (Child Pugh A, Child Pugh B), exposure to nintedanib was increased [see Clinical Pharmacology (12.3)] . In patients with mild hepatic impairment (Child Pugh A), the recommended dosage of OFEV is 100 mg twice daily [see Dosage and Administration (2.3)] . Monitor for adverse reactions and consider treatment interruption, or discontinuation for management of adverse reactions in these patients [see Dosage and Administration (2.4)]. Treatment of patients with moderate (Child Pugh B) and severe (Child Pugh C) hepatic impairment with OFEV is not recommended [see Warnings and Precautions (5.1)]." ;
    kg:token_count 186 .

<urn:kg:ofev:chunk:c9378762-438b-4e4e-9608-7b6a1c4a6f62> a kg:Chunk ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:chunk_id "c9378762-438b-4e4e-9608-7b6a1c4a6f62" ;
    kg:chunk_index 92 ;
    kg:extraction_method "docling" ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 14 ;
    kg:section_id "7f19e7ea-b8d2-4b24-9b58-e2ef9e2a8d0e" ;
    kg:source_document_id "c0db5dd8-583e-47bb-9b75-58402a32791c" ;
    kg:source_document_type "prescribing_information" ;
    kg:source_file_name "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:source_hash "e2c202ab3321472ab62bb6eb3470f3da540e15243d4cf97e48fb523742348f39" ;
    kg:text "In a study in renal cell cancer patients, QT/QTc measurements were recorded and showed that a single oral dose of 200 mg nintedanib as well as multiple oral doses of 200 mg nintedanib administered twice daily for 15 days did not prolong the QTcF interval." ;
    kg:token_count 64 .

<urn:kg:ofev:chunk:c9970eeb-0fa3-4ca6-82f8-15f9ff4f9b86> a kg:Chunk ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_Brand-Book_Nov-2015.pdf" ;
