<urn:kg:ofev:chunk:eba2f924-430f-4010-b05c-8b27364f5d21> a kg:Chunk ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:chunk_id "eba2f924-430f-4010-b05c-8b27364f5d21" ;
    kg:chunk_index 94 ;
    kg:extraction_method "docling" ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 15 ;
    kg:section_id "6935b2d8-a58f-4cd7-8833-6cf17d976232" ;
    kg:source_document_id "c0db5dd8-583e-47bb-9b75-58402a32791c" ;
    kg:source_document_type "prescribing_information" ;
    kg:source_file_name "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:source_hash "3b802a9f8483d86b7a040ac7380ca409a5c1809217e4f2ead160a64816b314c6" ;
    kg:text """Nintedanib reached maximum plasma concentrations approximately 2 to 4 hours after oral administration as a soft gelatin capsule under fed conditions. The absolute bioavailability of a 100 mg dose was 4.7% (90% CI: 3.62 to 6.08) in healthy volunteers. Absorption and bioavailability are decreased by transporter effects and substantial first-pass metabolism.

After food intake, nintedanib exposure increased by approximately 20% compared to administration under fasted conditions (90% CI: 95.3% to 152.5%) and absorption was delayed (median tmax fasted: 2.00 hours; fed: 3.98 hours), irrespective of the food type.""" ;
    kg:token_count 154 .

<urn:kg:ofev:chunk:ec588503-a85b-4bcb-8500-2c3fe93d7623> a kg:Chunk ;
    rdfs:label "ofev-epar-medicines-overview_en.pdf" ;
    kg:chunk_id "ec588503-a85b-4bcb-8500-2c3fe93d7623" ;
    kg:chunk_index 4 ;
    kg:extraction_method "docling" ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 2 ;
    kg:section_id "13760ddc-2921-4e57-b3f1-f2f10da5c57a" ;
    kg:source_document_id "dcf1cfdd-57dc-4936-b9b2-0839e9684cb2" ;
    kg:source_document_type "product_information" ;
    kg:source_file_name "ofev-epar-medicines-overview_en.pdf" ;
    kg:source_hash "cae49612b90887b1a288b918256c84d403496a94ad96225bcc143e6a4bd8ec30" ;
    kg:text """Ofev has been compared with placebo (a dummy treatment) in four main studies involving a total of 1,066 adults with IPF, 580 adults with systemic sclerosis-associated ILD and 663 adults with progressive fibrosing ILD. In all studies, the main measure of effectiveness was the decline in the functioning of the patients' lungs over the course of 1 year of treatment, measured by their forced vital capacity (FVC). FVC is the maximum amount of air a person can breathe out forcefully after taking in a deep breath and decreases as the condition gets worse.

In 2 studies involving adults with IPF, patients taking Ofev had a smaller decline in FVC than patients taking placebo, meaning that Ofev slowed down the worsening of the condition. The average FVC before treatment was between 2,600 and 2,700 millilitres (ml). In the first study, the average decrease in FVC over 1 year was 115 ml in patients taking Ofev compared with 240 ml in patients taking placebo. In the second study, the average decrease was 114 ml for Ofev compared with 207 ml for placebo. A further analysis of the results of the 2 main studies, which took into account that some patients stopped treatment, confirmed the benefits o""" ;
    kg:token_count 300 .

