<urn:kg:ofev:section:72235e47-f19c-4d6d-9129-d75e2319f70d> a kg:Section ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:SECTION_HAS_CHUNK <urn:kg:ofev:chunk:7b1e5716-779f-4d41-9eb5-e5e9dfc3e674>,
        <urn:kg:ofev:chunk:ba6eac01-9b7b-4e49-a153-b129edbfa52a> ;
    kg:extraction_method "docling" ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 7 ;
    kg:section_id "72235e47-f19c-4d6d-9129-d75e2319f70d" ;
    kg:section_title "5.7  Gastrointestinal Perforation" ;
    kg:section_type "heading" ;
    kg:source_document_id "c0db5dd8-583e-47bb-9b75-58402a32791c" ;
    kg:source_document_type "prescribing_information" ;
    kg:source_file_name "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:text """Based on the mechanism of action, OFEV may increase the risk of gastrointestinal perforation. In IPF studies (Study 1, Study 2, and Study 3), gastrointestinal perforation was reported in less than 1% of patients treated with OFEV, compared to 0 cases in the placebo-treated patients. In the chronic fibrosing ILDs with a progressive phenotype study (Study 5), gastrointestinal perforation was not reported in any patients in any treatment arm. In the SSc-ILD study (Study 4), no cases of gastrointestinal perforation were reported in patients treated with OFEV or in placebo-treated patients.

In the postmarketing period, cases of gastrointestinal perforations have been reported, some of which were fatal.

Use caution when treating patients who have had recent abdominal surgery, previous history of diverticular disease or receiving concomitant corticosteroids or NSAIDs. Discontinue therapy with OFEV in patients who develop gastrointestinal perforation. Only use OFEV in patients with known risk of gastrointestinal perforation if the anticipated benefit outweighs the potential risk.""" .

<urn:kg:ofev:section:74ba7b62-d92b-4327-9ba4-7052c1b100ec> a kg:Section ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_Brand-Book_Nov-2015.pdf" ;
    kg:SECTION_HAS_CHUNK <urn:kg:ofev:chunk:e0f6aea7-130d-4083-9fe3-50223fa0d459> ;
    kg:extraction_method "docling" ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 11 ;
    kg:section_id "74ba7b62-d92b-4327-9ba4-7052c1b100ec" ;
    kg:section_title "Logo" ;
    kg:section_type "heading" ;
    kg:source_document_id "7c99d579-1450-4667-9bc5-7d13421ddab2" ;
    kg:source_document_type "brand_book" ;
    kg:source_file_name "OFEV_Brand-Book_Nov-2015.pdf" ;
    kg:text """The OFEV ®  logo consists of 3 elements: the logotype, the symbol, and the generic name. The symbol depicts wings in motion that dependably guard patients. The symbol is inspiring and representational of high performance because of its forward motion. The use of dark blue and all caps in the logotype communicate reliability and vigilance.

The logo should never be changed in any way to keep the personality consistent worldwide.

uS LoGo

<!-- image -->

Some markets have regulations about the size relationship between the brand and generic names. the united States, for which the oFEV ®  logo is illustrated here, has very speciffc rules regarding that relationship. Please use the assets that have been provided and approved for your region.

<!-- image -->""" .

<urn:kg:ofev:section:769f8a50-7997-4e9d-bef1-a063ec1f3191> a kg:Section ;
    rdfs:label "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:SECTION_HAS_CHUNK <urn:kg:ofev:chunk:843021b2-839a-453e-8e3e-cff396d2fbdf> ;
    kg:extraction_method "docling" ;
    kg:graphScope "brand" ;
    kg:page_number 31 ;
    kg:section_id "769f8a50-7997-4e9d-bef1-a063ec1f3191" ;
    kg:section_title "Before you take OFEV, tell your doctor about all of your medical conditions, including if you:" ;
    kg:section_type "heading" ;
    kg:source_document_id "c0db5dd8-583e-47bb-9b75-58402a32791c" ;
    kg:source_document_type "prescribing_information" ;
    kg:source_file_name "OFEV_PI.pdf" ;
    kg:text """- have liver problems.
- have heart problems.
- have a history of blood clots.
- have a bleeding problem or a family history of a bleeding problem.
- have had recent surgery in your stomach (abdominal) area.
- are a smoker.
- are pregnant or plan to become pregnant. OFEV can harm your unborn baby. OFEV can cause birth defects or death to an unborn baby. See 'What is the most important information I should know about OFEV?'
- are breastfeeding or plan to breastfeed. It is not known if OFEV passes into your breast milk. You should not breastfeed while taking OFEV.

Tell your doctor about all the medicines you take, including prescription and over-the-counter medicines, vitamins, and herbal supplements such as St. John's wort. Keep a list of the medicines you take and show it to your doctor and pharmacist when you get a new medicine.""" .

